     1	# DaoStream Design
     2	
     3	Status: agreed design target. This document is the DaoStream contract. It does
     4	not redesign Yin.VM. Yin.VM is a downstream consumer and will be addressed only
     5	after this contract is implemented and tested.
     6	
     7	## Purpose
     8	
     9	This contract derives from the foundational axioms and non-negotiable
    10	invariants of [`datom.world.md`](./datom.world.md). Two axioms bear on it
    11	directly, named here once so the sections below can cite rather than restate
    12	them:
    13	
    14	1. **Everything is a Stream** — all IO and data flow through append-only
    15	   streams.
    16	2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics** — data is syntax; semantics emerge
    17	   only through interpretation. One truth, many perspectives.
    18	
    19	DaoStream is the passive substrate for IO in datom.world — passive because
    20	interpretation and execution must not collapse into one layer. Axiom 1 is
    21	what makes this the layer every host boundary crosses. A stream carries
    22	values and decides nothing about them:
    23	
    24	- **What the values mean** is not its business — Axiom 2. An element
    25	  judged, filtered, or typed at this layer destroys the perspectives nobody
    26	  has taken yet.
    27	- **Which interpreter observes them** is not recorded — a registry of who
    28	  is listening would be hidden global state.
    29	- **When an interpreter runs** is not its decision — "do not introduce
    30	  callbacks; every callback is events on a stream", seen from the stream's
    31	  side: DaoStream invokes nothing, so control is never inverted and
    32	  causality stays explicit (see The IO Model).
    33	
    34	The Invariants below are these denials in operational form.
    35	
    36	The design separates four concepts:
    37	
    38	1. A **creation specification** is data for creating a new logical stream.
    39	2. A **handle** is a host-local operational stream value — the
    40	   implementation of the `dao.stream` protocols.
    41	3. A **portable descriptor** is data used to attach to an existing stream.
    42	4. A **cursor** is immutable interpreter-owned observation state for one
    43	   logical stream.
    44	
    45	Creation is not attachment. A descriptor is not a creation specification. A
    46	handle is not portable identity. A cursor is not handle-owned state.
    47	
    48	Seven operations range over them, each returning an outcome map (see
    49	Result Convention):
    50	
    51	| Operation    | Consumes               | Yields             |
    52	|--------------|------------------------|--------------------|
    53	| `create!`    | creation specification | handle outcome     |
    54	| `attach!`    | portable descriptor    | handle outcome     |
    55	| `descriptor` | handle                 | descriptor outcome |
    56	| `cursor`     | handle, anchor         | cursor outcome     |
    57	| `next`       | handle, cursor         | read outcome       |
    58	| `append!`    | handle, value          | write outcome      |
    59	| `close!`     | handle                 | close outcome      |
    60	
    61	A handle implements the subset of these operations its transport
    62	declares (see Surfaces).
    63	
    64	## Invariants
    65	
    66	- Stream elements are data. DaoStream assigns them no intrinsic meaning.
    67	- Reading is non-destructive. A reader advances its own cursor, not the stream.
    68	- Multiple cursors may observe the same retained value independently.
    69	- Interpreters own cursors and decide when to call `next`.
    70	- A runtime may drive an interpreter, but DaoStream does not require a runtime.
    71	- Closing a stream changes future availability but does not itself erase
    72	  retained history.
    73	- Retention may be bounded. A cursor over evicted history receives a gap
    74	  outcome as data; the reader decides what missing data means.
    75	- All expected operational outcomes are data, not exceptions or callbacks.
    76	
    77	Every clause below derives from these invariants. A proposed addition that
    78	cannot be derived from one of them does not belong in this contract.
    79	
    80	## Result Convention
    81	
    82	Every DaoStream operation returns data.
    83	
    84	- Each operation has a closed set of outcomes. An outcome is a map whose
    85	  `:dao.stream/outcome` key names the outcome with a qualified keyword. Every
    86	  operation returns such a map — including projections like `descriptor` and
    87	  `cursor`, whose success map carries the projected value.
    88	- Result maps are **open**: the contract fixes which keys are required per
    89	  outcome, and a consumer must ignore keys it does not understand. This is
    90	  what allows future extensions (for example readiness notification) to be
    91	  added without breaking existing consumers.
    92	- Exceptions are reserved for defects in the host's own assembly of
    93	  DaoStream, detected before any operation runs. Everything observable at an
    94	  operation returns data: closed, full, blocked, end, gap, not found,
    95	  malformed input, and transport defects are all outcomes, never exceptions
    96	  and never callbacks.
    97	- All qualified keywords in results live under `:dao.stream/…`. (The legacy
    98	  `:daostream/gap` spelling is retired.)
    99	
   100	## The IO Model
   101	
   102	Three models are available for expressing IO. DaoStream is deliberately the
   103	third.
   104	
   105	**Blocking (synchronous) IO.** The operation parks until it has an answer,
   106	then returns it. Control flow stays sequential and the call site reads as
   107	ordinary code, which is the whole of its appeal. The cost is that something
   108	must wait — a thread per pending operation, held for its duration — and it
   109	requires a host that can park at all.
   110	
   111	**Callback (asynchronous) IO.** The operation registers a continuation and
   112	returns at once; the runtime invokes the continuation when the answer
   113	arrives. Nothing waits, so one thread carries many pending operations: this
   114	is the model that scales. Its cost is inverted control. The caller no longer
   115	decides when it observes the answer, and the inversion is viral — on a host
   116	without blocking, a promise cannot be absorbed inside a function, because
   117	awaiting returns to the caller at the first suspension point and makes the
   118	caller's own result a promise in turn. Whatever is reached from a callback
   119	is reached from a callback.
   120	
   121	**Non-blocking polling.** The operation returns immediately with whatever is
   122	true now, including the answer "nothing yet, ask again." Nothing parks and
   123	nothing is inverted: the caller keeps control and decides when to ask again.
   124	This is POSIX `O_NONBLOCK` without `select`, and it is DaoStream's model.
   125	
   126	`next` is total and non-blocking (see Reading): every state of the stream
   127	has a defined data answer, and `:dao.stream/blocked` is `EAGAIN` — this
   128	position holds nothing yet. `append!`'s `:dao.stream/full` is the same
   129	answer for writes. No operation in this contract parks, and none takes a
   130	callback; the Result Convention's "never exceptions and never callbacks" is
   131	this model stated as a rule about results.
   132	
   133	The rule generalizes past `next`: **no operation waits.** An operation that
   134	cannot yet know its answer returns the outcome true at the moment it is
   135	called, and the eventual answer arrives the way every other fact does — as
   136	data, observed later, through an ordinary operation or on a stream. A
   137	DaoStream operation is never a request for something to happen and be
   138	reported back. It is a question about now.
   139	
   140	### Why this model and not the other two
   141	
   142	The hosts do not offer the same choices. The JVM has both blocking and
   143	asynchronous IO. JavaScript and Dart have only the asynchronous kind: there
   144	is no blocking read to call and no way to wait for one, because the single
   145	event loop that would deliver the answer is the one a waiting call would be
   146	occupying — waiting deadlocks rather than merely costing a thread.
   147	
   148	A blocking contract is therefore unimplementable on most of the targets. A
   149	callback contract is implementable on all of them and inverts control on all
   150	of them, including the host that never needed it. Non-blocking polling is
   151	the only one of the three that means the same thing everywhere — the same
   152	operations, the same outcome sets, the same control flow — which is what
   153	lets one surface serve clj, cljs, and cljd without the shape of the API
   154	differing by host.
   155	
   156	### What it costs
   157	
   158	Polling without readiness notification is O(n) in streams observed: a reader
   159	holding ten thousand idle streams must ask all ten thousand to learn which
   160	have data. This is `select` without `select`, and it is a real limit rather
   161	than an oversight — the extension that answers it is reserved below. The
   162	cost also lands where it can be paid: DaoStream schedules nothing, so
   163	cadence belongs to the runtime driving the interpreters, and a readiness
   164	mechanism belongs there too.
   165	
   166	### The readiness extension
   167	
   168	This contract has no readiness or waiter extension. A reader that receives
   169	`:dao.stream/blocked` retries `next` when it chooses; retry cadence is the
   170	concern of the interpreter or the runtime driving it, not of the stream.
   171	
   172	A future readiness extension would be additive: an optional protocol on
   173	transports that support it, plus optional keys in operation results (for
   174	example a wake token list on `append!`), which the open-map rule already
   175	permits. Nothing in this contract needs to change for that; no consumer of
   176	this contract may depend on it existing.
   177	
   178	## Creation and Attachment
   179	
   180	Creation brings a new logical stream into existence. Attachment joins an
   181	existing one. They are separate operations with separate inputs.
   182	
   183	- `create!` consumes a creation specification and returns a handle on a
   184	  new logical stream. The handle's portable identity is available as a
   185	  descriptor via `descriptor`.
   186	- `attach!` consumes a portable descriptor and returns a handle on the
   187	  **same** logical stream the descriptor names. Attaching never creates.
   188	- A creation specification says how to build a stream. A descriptor only
   189	  names one — never executable code, never a construction program, never a
   190	  request to load a plugin.
   191	
   192	Streams are values that can be sent through streams — up to a serialization
   193	boundary. An in-memory stream holds references, so a handle can ride it
   194	intact, and an interpreter that receives a handle simply uses it —
   195	every operation takes a handle, and attaching to what you already hold
   196	is meaningless. But a handle does not survive encoding, and any
   197	transport that encodes its elements — a file as much as a network socket —
   198	is a serialization boundary. Descriptors exist for crossing those
   199	boundaries, where the live value cannot travel and a name must. (What a
   200	transport can carry is transport-owned: handing an encoding transport a
   201	value it cannot encode is reported as data like any other outcome.)
   202	`attach!` therefore consumes only descriptors, and
   203	resolves them against the transport's inherent operational state — the
   204	streams a network endpoint serves because serving them is what it is, the
   205	files a filesystem holds. There is no stream table in this contract: no
   206	registry maps descriptors to live handles, so a descriptor arriving
   207	somewhere with nothing behind it — a ring buffer's descriptor on any other
   208	host, or on its own host absent a directory some composition chose to keep —
   209	honestly resolves to `:dao.stream/not-found`.
   210	
   211	### Envelopes
   212	
   213	A **creation specification** is a map with one required key:
   214	
   215	- `:dao.stream/type` — a qualified keyword naming the transport. This is the
   216	  dispatch key.
   217	
   218	Every other key is transport-owned, qualified under the transport's namespace
   219	(for example a ring buffer's capacity), and validated by the selected handler
   220	— generic validation checks only the envelope, and a handler that rejects its
   221	own keys returns `:dao.stream/invalid-spec`.
   222	
   223	The **portable descriptor** is inspired by CORBA's interoperable object
   224	reference. Its exact definition is not designed yet; these properties are
   225	settled, and the key set that realizes them is TBD:
   226	
   227	- It carries `:dao.stream/type` — the transport, as above — as its dispatch
   228	  key.
   229	- It names exactly one logical stream, and that identity is stable: the same
   230	  descriptor attaches to the same stream every time.
   231	- It is self-contained. If the stream is remote, it contains whatever is
   232	  needed to connect — perhaps an IP address and port. The entry data is
   233	  transport-owned; a handler that cannot make sense of it returns
   234	  `:dao.stream/invalid-descriptor`.
   235	- Like an IOR, it may carry authorization: an optional `:dao.stream/shibi`
   236	  key holding Shibi capability tokens that gate whether an interpreter may
   237	  attach. Shibi is not yet designed or built; until it is, the key is
   238	  reserved, no attachment is gated, and the `:dao.stream/denied` outcome
   239	  reserved in the `attach!` table below is unreachable.
   240	
   241	For the time being, the operative guarantee is the round trip: `descriptor`
   242	on a live handle returns `:dao.stream/ok` with `:dao.stream/descriptor`,
   243	a value that can be sent on a stream and reconstructed on the other end,
   244	where `attach!` on the reconstructed value reaches the stream it names —
   245	provided the receiving host can reach what it names at all (see Creation and
   246	Attachment on reachability).
   247	
   248	Both envelopes are plain data through and through: every value must survive
   249	the host serialization codec structurally unchanged, so equality is
   250	structural, and no key holds a function, a host object, a live handle, or
   251	identity smuggled in metadata. Like every DaoStream map, envelopes are open —
   252	a consumer ignores qualified keys it does not understand.
   253	
   254	`create!` outcomes:
   255	
   256	| Outcome                       | Meaning                                       | Required keys        |
   257	|-------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------|----------------------|
   258	| `:dao.stream/ok`              | A new logical stream exists.                  | `:dao.stream/handle` |
   259	| `:dao.stream/invalid-spec`    | The creation specification is malformed.      | —                    |
   260	| `:dao.stream/not-found`       | No transport here matches `:dao.stream/type`. | —                    |
   261	| `:dao.stream/transport-error` | The handler failed.                           | —                    |
   262	
   263	`attach!` outcomes:
   264	
   265	| Outcome                          | Meaning                                                                             | Required keys        |
   266	|----------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------|
   267	| `:dao.stream/ok`                 | Attached to the existing logical stream.                                            | `:dao.stream/handle` |
   268	| `:dao.stream/invalid-descriptor` | The descriptor is malformed.                                                        | —                    |
   269	| `:dao.stream/not-found`          | The descriptor names no stream here, or no transport matches its type.              | —                    |
   270	| `:dao.stream/denied`             | Attachment refused by capability gating (reserved; unreachable until Shibi exists). | —                    |
   271	| `:dao.stream/transport-error`    | The handler failed.                                                                 | —                    |
   272	
   273	### Host Dispatch
   274	
   275	When a creation specification or portable descriptor arrives dynamically —
   276	for example off a stream — the host looks at it and dispatches on
   277	`:dao.stream/type`. The type resolves to a map that has the transport's
   278	`:dao.stream/create` and `:dao.stream/attach` implementations:
   279	
   280	```clojure
   281	{:dao.stream/ringbuffer {:dao.stream/create ringbuffer/create!
   282	                         :dao.stream/attach ringbuffer/attach!}
   283	 :dao.stream/ws         {:dao.stream/attach ws/attach!}}
   284	```
   285	
   286	The entries are per-transport and may be partial. A transport that creates
   287	no streams supplies no `:dao.stream/create` — the WebSocket transport owns
   288	no stream, it serves streams the host composed, so nothing there creates
   289	one. A creation specification naming it finds no create implementation and
   290	returns `:dao.stream/not-found`, the same answer as an absent transport and
   291	the correct one: nothing here creates that.
   292	
   293	This table is ordinary data owned by the host, and the dispatch is an
   294	ordinary map lookup done by the host — DaoStream neither performs nor
   295	standardizes it. A host that cannot dispatch on `:dao.stream/type` returns
   296	`:dao.stream/not-found` — never creation or discovery.
   297	
   298	Code that knows its transport statically calls the transport's functions
   299	directly; no dispatch is involved. There is no ambient namespace-global
   300	registry and no namespace-load registration side effect. (The legacy `open!`
   301	multimethod and `defopen` macro are retired.)
   302	
   303	## Surfaces
   304	
   305	A handle implements the subset of the public surface its transport can
   306	honor — **reader** (`cursor`, `next`), **writer** (`append!`), **closable**
   307	(`close!`) — and the transport declares which. The Cursors, Reading, and
   308	Retention and Gaps sections govern handles with a reader surface only.
   309	
   310	The reader surface is a promise: the handle presents its elements as
   311	one positioned, append-only, retained sequence that cursors can observe and
   312	re-observe. A transport whose medium retains nothing cannot make that
   313	promise and has no reader surface: its inbound events are deposited by the
   314	host's adapter onto the stream the host composition wired for that boundary
   315	— where the reading model applies in full. The log lives beside such a
   316	transport, composed explicitly by the host, never hidden inside the
   317	transport. A deposit destination must admit every valid boundary event per
   318	its declared retention; wiring one that can refuse is a host assembly
   319	defect. (`dao.stream.ws.md` specifies one such transport and the admission
   320	rule in detail.)
   321	
   322	### Where the asynchrony goes
   323	
   324	The IO Model makes this contract non-blocking; hosts are asynchronous
   325	whether or not it is. A network message arrives when the runtime says so, in
   326	a callback, on a thread or event loop DaoStream does not own. DaoStream does
   327	not pretend otherwise; it confines the inversion to one place. The host's
   328	boundary adapter *is* callback code, and its entire job is to `append!` what
   329	arrived. Past that append the event holds a position in a sequence, and
   330	every reader above observes it with its own cursor, in its own control flow,
   331	when it chooses.
   332	
   333	This is an un-inversion. The real cost of callback IO is not performance but
   334	that the inversion spreads through everything it touches; depositing into a
   335	retained sequence pays that cost once, at the boundary, and returns ordinary
   336	code to everyone above. The reader-surface rule above is the same thing
   337	stated structurally: a transport that retains nothing has no reader surface,
   338	and its inbound events are deposited onto a stream that does.
   339	
   340	## Cursors
   341	
   342	A cursor is an immutable value owned by the interpreter that holds it.
   343	
   344	- Cursors are minted by the stream: `cursor` on a handle takes an **anchor**
   345	  and returns `:dao.stream/ok` with `:dao.stream/cursor`. The contract
   346	  defines two anchors — `:dao.stream/oldest`, the earliest retained
   347	  position, and `:dao.stream/newest`, positioned after the newest appended
   348	  value so the first `next` observes the next value to arrive. A transport
   349	  whose medium is naturally addressable may accept additional
   350	  transport-owned anchors (a byte offset, a timestamp). An anchor that is
   351	  malformed or not honored by this transport yields
   352	  `:dao.stream/invalid-anchor`. On a handle whose attachment has closed,
   353	  `cursor` yields `:dao.stream/closed`; a handle on the logical stream
   354	  itself keeps minting cursors after close, so retained history remains
   355	  readable.
   356	- Every valid cursor comes from the stream: minted by `cursor`, received as
   357	  a successor from `next`, or recovered from a gap outcome. Consumers never
   358	  construct cursor internals or fabricate positions — there is no `seek`. A
   359	  cursor's representation belongs to the transport.
   360	- A cursor binds to the logical stream, not to a handle: any handle on the
   361	  same logical stream accepts it, so a cursor handed to another interpreter
   362	  works with that interpreter's own handle. Whether a cursor survives
   363	  serialization to another host is transport-owned and TBD, like the
   364	  descriptor envelope.
   365	- A cursor carries enough identity to detect misuse — the mismatch outcomes
   366	  in the Reading table depend on it.
   367	- Advancing is receiving a successor cursor from `next`. The stream never
   368	  advances a reader; the stream holds no reader positions at all. Dropping a
   369	  cursor is how a reader detaches — there is nothing to unregister.
   370	- Because cursors are immutable values, a reader may retain an old cursor and
   371	  re-read retained history, or hand a cursor to another interpreter. What a
   372	  cursor can still observe is governed only by retention.
   373	
   374	## Reading
   375	
   376	`next` takes a handle and a cursor and returns an outcome immediately.
   377	It is total and non-blocking: every state of the stream has a defined data
   378	answer.
   379	
   380	| Outcome                       | Meaning                                                                       | Required keys                                                                           |
   381	|-------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
   382	| `:dao.stream/ok`              | A value was observed.                                                         | `:dao.stream/value`, `:dao.stream/cursor` (successor)                                   |
   383	| `:dao.stream/blocked`         | Nothing at this position yet; retry later.                                    | —                                                                                       |
   384	| `:dao.stream/end`             | What this handle is on is closed and this position is past the retained tail. | —                                                                                       |
   385	| `:dao.stream/gap`             | This position was evicted.                                                    | `:dao.stream/cursor` (earliest retained position, or the tail when nothing is retained) |
   386	| `:dao.stream/cursor-mismatch` | The cursor was minted by a different logical stream.                          | —                                                                                       |
   387	| `:dao.stream/invalid-cursor`  | The value is not a cursor.                                                    | —                                                                                       |
   388	
   389	Reading never mutates the stream. Two readers with cursors at the same
   390	position observe the same retained value; neither can starve or affect the
   391	other. There is no destructive read anywhere in the contract — no drain, no
   392	take. An operation that removes an element as it reads would make one
   393	reader's progress every other reader's data loss.
   394	
   395	## Writing
   396	
   397	`append!` takes a handle and a value and returns an outcome:
   398	
   399	| Outcome                     | Meaning                                                                                                                     | Required keys |
   400	|-----------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|---------------|
   401	| `:dao.stream/ok`            | The value is accepted at the next position in the stream's sequence. Implies neither local readability nor remote delivery. | —             |
   402	| `:dao.stream/full`          | The transport cannot accept the value now; nothing was appended.                                                            | —             |
   403	| `:dao.stream/invalid-value` | The transport cannot carry this value (for example, it cannot be encoded); nothing was appended.                            | —             |
   404	| `:dao.stream/closed`        | What this handle is on is closed to new appends; the value was not appended.                                                | —             |
   405	
   406	The writer decides what to do about full or closed; DaoStream does not retry,
   407	buffer, or notify on its behalf.
   408	
   409	## Close
   410	
   411	`close!` takes a handle and returns a map whose outcome is
   412	`:dao.stream/ok`. It is idempotent: closing an already-closed scope is also
   413	`:dao.stream/ok`.
   414	
   415	`close!` closes **what the handle is on**. A handle that owns a stream — its
   416	creator, or its server-side host — closes the logical stream. An attached
   417	handle is on an attachment: closing it ends that attachment only — the
   418	stream persists, its descriptor remains valid, and `attach!` may rejoin
   419	later. Which scope a transport's handles carry is part of its declared
   420	nature.
   421	
   422	Outcome meanings are handle-relative throughout: `:dao.stream/closed` and
   423	`:dao.stream/end` speak of what *this handle* is on. After an attachment
   424	closes, the handle's `append!` answers `closed`; `descriptor` still answers
   425	`ok` — identity outlives any attachment; a reader surface, where the
   426	handle has one, answers `end` from `next` once observation through this
   427	handle is exhausted, and `closed` from `cursor` — no new cursors are
   428	minted through a closed attachment. Cursors bind to the logical stream,
   429	not to a handle, so a kept cursor resumes through a newly attached handle.
   430	
   431	Closing the logical stream changes future availability only:
   432	
   433	- Subsequent `append!` returns `:dao.stream/closed`.
   434	- A reader that exhausts retained history receives `:dao.stream/end` instead
   435	  of `:dao.stream/blocked`.
   436	- Retained history remains readable. Close erases nothing; eviction is a
   437	  retention concern, not a lifecycle one.
   438	
   439	There is no `closed?` predicate in the public surface. Operation results are
   440	authoritative; a predicate answer is stale the moment it is returned.
   441	
   442	## Concurrency
   443	
   444	Each logical stream is one append-only sequence: a successfully appended
   445	value occupies exactly one definite position in that sequence, and every
   446	operation's outcome reflects the sequence at one moment. DaoStream
   447	guarantees nothing further about concurrent operations.
   448	
   449	This follows from Axiom 2. The sequence is the one truth; each outcome is
   450	one perspective on it at one moment. A reader that observes
   451	`:dao.stream/blocked` and then a value has not caught the stream in a
   452	contradiction — it made two observations from its own cursor, and no global
   453	view exists to contradict. Whether an append landed before a close is
   454	answered by the sequence itself: the value has a position or it does not.
   455	What an ordering between writers *means*, and any coordination among them —
   456	a merge interpreter, a one-log-per-writer discipline — is interpretation,
   457	and interpretation happens above the stream.
   458	
   459	## Retention and Gaps
   460	
   461	Retention and overflow behavior are transport-owned, declared by the
   462	creation specification. A ring buffer retains a bounded window and evicts
   463	the oldest; a file or append-only log retains everything; a network send
   464	buffer refuses transiently. Which outcomes a given stream can produce —
   465	`full` on append, `gap` on read — and whether its `full` is transient or
   466	permanent follow from the transport's declared nature. DaoStream names the
   467	outcomes and standardizes no policy.
   468	
   469	When bounded retention evicts a value, a cursor still pointing at it gets the
   470	gap outcome from the Reading table. The stream reports honestly and decides
   471	nothing: an interpreter replaying a log may treat a gap as fatal; a live
   472	telemetry viewer may adopt the returned cursor and continue. Silently
   473	skipping evicted history is forbidden — a reader must be able to know it
   474	missed values.
   475	
   476	## Composition
   477	
   478	DaoStream defines only streams, cursors, and the operations above. Anything
   479	that transforms one stream into another is an interpreter — it reads via its
   480	own cursor and appends to an ordinary output stream — and that lives entirely
   481	outside this contract, needing no support from it. As in a Unix pipeline, the
   482	pipe is dumb; the transforming happens in the processes on either end.
   483	
   484	## Explicitly Absent
   485	
   486	Absent from the public surface, by derivation from the invariants: `open!`
   487	and `defopen` (ambient registry), `closed?` (stale predicate), `drain-one!`
   488	and any destructive read (readers advance cursors, not the stream), `seek`
   489	(every valid cursor comes from the stream; anchored minting and kept
   490	cursors cover repositioning), `take!!` (blocking), waiter registration
   491	(no readiness extension), and throwing conveniences such as `strict-vec`
   492	(operational outcomes are data).
     1	# DaoStream WebSocket Transport
     2	
     3	Status: design target, subordinate to `dao.stream.md`. That document is the
     4	contract; this one specifies the WebSocket transport's implementation of it.
     5	Where the two disagree, the contract wins.
     6	
     7	## What the Descriptor Names
     8	
     9	A WebSocket descriptor names a **server-hosted stream**, never a connection.
    10	The stream exists on the serving host — created there, retained there,
    11	closed there by its owner — and it exists whether or not any client is
    12	currently connected. A connection is the mechanics of one attachment:
    13	connecting joins the stream, disconnecting detaches from it, and neither
    14	event creates, closes, or destroys anything. That is what makes `attach!`
    15	honest here: the same descriptor reaches the same stream every time, because
    16	the stream's identity and lifecycle belong to the server, not to the wire.
    17	
    18	This framing generalizes: **serving is what makes any stream remotely
    19	attachable.** A ring buffer is host-local only because nothing speaks for
    20	it. A host that composes a WebSocket endpoint in front of one is serving
    21	that ring buffer; its descriptor now carries an address, and remote
    22	interpreters can attach. The transport owns no stream — it serves streams
    23	the host composed, which is exactly the contract's rule that descriptors
    24	resolve against "the streams a network endpoint serves because serving them
    25	is what it is."
    26	
    27	A server that wants per-conversation state does not bend attachment into
    28	creation: it `create!`s a fresh stream for the conversation and offers that
    29	stream's descriptor.
    30	
    31	## Surfaces
    32	
    33	A WebSocket handle is **writer** and **closable** only. It has no
    34	reader surface: a socket retains nothing, so it cannot make the reader
    35	surface's promise — a positioned, append-only, retained sequence that
    36	cursors can observe and re-observe. `cursor` and `next` do not exist on it.
    37	
    38	## The Duplex Model
    39	
    40	A WebSocket is two independent directions sharing one connection, and the
    41	design keeps them separate:
    42	
    43	- **Outbound**: `append!` on the socket handle encodes the value and
    44	  sends it. Sending is appending; nothing is converted.
    45	- **Inbound**: each arriving message is a host event. The host's adapter
    46	  deposits it — payloads and connection lifecycle events alike, judging
    47	  nothing — onto the stream the **host composition** wired for this
    48	  boundary. Per ADR 0003 there is no separate event bus: dao.space is the
    49	  medium. From that moment the traffic has positions, retention, cursors,
    50	  and gaps — the reading model of the contract applies in full, on that
    51	  stream.
    52	
    53	The deposit destination is wired once, when the host composes the boundary
    54	— it is not a parameter of `attach!`, and no attaching caller chooses or
    55	knows it. Deposited events carry the attachment's identity, so every
    56	interpreter takes its own perspective on the same deposited truth: one
    57	indexes the traffic, another copies it to a file, a third ignores it. How
    58	arrivals are interpreted is the interpreter's business, not the
    59	transport's configuration.
    60	
    61	```clojure
    62	(def sock (-> (ws/attach! {:dao.stream/type :dao.stream/ws
    63	                           :ws/host "example.org"
    64	                           :ws/port 9090
    65	                           :ws/path "/streams/telemetry"})
    66	              :dao.stream/handle))
    67	
    68	;; write: (append! sock msg)
    69	;; read:  interpreters observe the medium the host boundary deposits into,
    70	;;        filtering on this attachment's identity
    71	```
    72	
    73	Nothing accumulates inside the transport: a hidden per-socket inbox would
    74	be a retention window with an eviction policy nobody composed, sized by
    75	nobody. Retention lives in the medium the boundary deposits into, composed
    76	and sized by the host.
    77	
    78	There are no callback hooks. The legacy `on-open!` / `on-message!` /
    79	`on-close!` surface is retired: every socket event becomes data on the
    80	deposit stream — payload and lifecycle (opened, closed by peer, error)
    81	alike, distinguishable by shape. A boundary deposits everything; whether
    82	anyone reads it is not the boundary's concern.
    83	
    84	### Deposit Admission
    85	
    86	The adapter runs in a callback context: it cannot wait, cannot park, and
    87	cannot record a refused deposit in the medium that refused it. So the
    88	deposit destination must be one that always admits, and this is enforced
    89	where it can be — at assembly, against the destination's declared nature:
    90	
    91	- The destination must declare **evict-oldest retention with a
    92	  host-declared capacity**. It never answers `:dao.stream/full`; under
    93	  pressure, loss surfaces as reported `gap`s at lagging readers — the one
    94	  loss mode the contract blesses. Unbounded in-memory retention is not an
    95	  alternative: it trades a refused deposit for eventual host death, the
    96	  most silent failure of all. (A genuinely durable medium — a disk-backed
    97	  log — plays the same game with a much larger declared window; exhausting
    98	  it at runtime surfaces as `:dao.stream/transport-error` on the deposit,
    99	  like any storage failure.)
   100	- Wiring a reject-mode destination is a **host assembly defect**, refused
   101	  when the boundary is composed — the same category as supplying both
   102	  dispatch shapes. Throwing is reserved for defects detected there, before
   103	  the boundary operates; everything at deposit time is data.
   104	- The destination must outlive the boundary, and **every non-`ok` deposit
   105	  result tears the connection down**: `closed` means the destination is
   106	  gone; `full` or `invalid-value` from a supposedly conforming destination
   107	  is a host or transport defect, evidenced by the returned data;
   108	  `transport-error` is runtime storage failure. In every case, a boundary
   109	  that can no longer turn events into data stops claiming to be attached.
   110	  Disconnection is fully observable — the peer's boundary deposits the
   111	  departure, the local handle answers `:dao.stream/closed`, and
   112	  re-attaching after the host repairs its composition is ordinary
   113	  `attach!`.
   114	
   115	Sizing the window is choosing how much reader lag to tolerate before gaps
   116	appear — an explicit engineering decision in the host's composition. Where
   117	the destination transport supports it, the window may be resized live
   118	(a transport-owned operation on the owning handle): growing it only slows
   119	the eviction horizon, shrinking it is ordinary early eviction reported as
   120	gaps, and positions never renumber. An adaptive policy — grow on rising
   121	gap rate, shrink when quiet — is an interpreter reading its own telemetry,
   122	not a transport behavior.
   123	
   124	The accounting is then complete: overload is a reported `gap`, a dead
   125	destination is an observable disconnection, and silent loss is
   126	unconstructible.
   127	
   128	## Envelope
   129	
   130	Transport-owned keys, qualified under `:ws/…`. The exact key set is TBD
   131	alongside the portable descriptor's definition in the contract; settled
   132	properties:
   133	
   134	- The descriptor is self-contained and exists to cross the network: it
   135	  carries what any host needs to reach the served stream — host and port,
   136	  plus the served stream's identity (a path or stream id; endpoint address
   137	  alone does not name a stream), perhaps TLS parameters.
   138	- The descriptor carries identity only. Where inbound traffic lands is the
   139	  host boundary's composition (see The Duplex Model) and appears in no
   140	  envelope and no argument of `attach!`.
   141	
   142	## Operations
   143	
   144	- There is no `create!`. The transport owns no stream, so it creates none;
   145	  a host serves a stream it composed elsewhere, and starting an endpoint is
   146	  a host composition step, not a DaoStream operation. A creation
   147	  specification naming this transport returns `:dao.stream/not-found`.
   148	- `attach!` begins connecting and returns without waiting, per the
   149	  contract's rule that no operation waits. It answers what is decidable
   150	  locally, at the moment it is called: `:dao.stream/invalid-descriptor` for
   151	  a descriptor it cannot read, otherwise `:dao.stream/ok` with a handle on
   152	  an attachment still being established. It never returns an answer it has
   153	  not heard. This is not a concession to convenience: on a host with no
   154	  blocking IO the endpoint's answer cannot arrive before `attach!` returns,
   155	  and an `attach!` that waited for it would have to hand back a promise —
   156	  breaking the result convention on three of four hosts and giving the same
   157	  transport different outcome sets per host.
   158	- **How the connection resolved arrives as data**, on the same host-composed
   159	  medium as every other socket event, correlated by attachment identity.
   160	  The distinction survives intact, only its channel moves:
   161	  `:dao.stream/not-found` is the authoritative answer that what the
   162	  descriptor names does not exist — the endpoint responded and disclaimed
   163	  the stream — and is not retried automatically (though a server may serve
   164	  that identity later; retrying on external evidence is interpreter
   165	  policy), while `:dao.stream/transport-error` covers transient
   166	  reachability and connection failures — DNS, timeout, routing, refusal —
   167	  and retrying it may succeed on its own. A caller's retry policy is as
   168	  deterministic as before; it reads the answer from the medium instead of
   169	  from a return value.
   170	- `append!` returns `:dao.stream/full` when the send buffer cannot accept
   171	  the value now — **transient**: the network drains the buffer on its own,
   172	  and a later append succeeds. An attachment still establishing its
   173	  connection answers `full` for the same reason and with the same remedy.
   174	  `:dao.stream/closed` once the connection is down, which includes an
   175	  attachment whose connection never succeeded. A value that cannot be
   176	  encoded for the wire is `:dao.stream/invalid-value`. Operating the
   177	  handle is therefore the second way a caller learns how the connection
   178	  resolved, and needs no reader at all.
   179	- `close!` on an attached handle means **disconnect from the server**: the
   180	  handle is on the attachment, so closing it ends the connection,
   181	  idempotently, and this handle's subsequent `append!` returns
   182	  `:dao.stream/closed`. The served stream is untouched — closing *it*
   183	  belongs to its server-side owner. Disconnection is not final: the
   184	  descriptor remains valid and `attach!` rejoins the same stream. The
   185	  peer's boundary deposits the departure into its host-composed event
   186	  medium, correlated by attachment identity.
   187	- Connection loss is the same detachment, uninvited: the served stream
   188	  persists, its descriptor remains valid, and `attach!` with the same
   189	  descriptor rejoins the same stream.
   190	
   191	## Elements and Serialization
   192	
   193	The wire is a serialization boundary. Every element must survive the codec
   194	structurally unchanged; a handle can never travel — its portable
   195	descriptor can.
   196	
   197	## Deferred
   198	
   199	- Exact envelope key set (tracks the contract's descriptor TBD).
   200	- Resumption protocol: stream identity is stable across reconnects, so
   201	  resumption is possible by design; what history a rejoining client receives
   202	  (and how it states where it left off) is not yet specified.
   203	- Backpressure protocols above the stream (a reader publishing its progress
   204	  as data is interpretation, outside both this transport and the contract).
   205	- Relationship to the RPC layers built over streams.
     1	# DaoStream Implementation Plan — the WebSocket Slice
     2	
     3	Status: migration plan, derived from and subordinate to `dao.stream.md`
     4	(the contract, review-approved) and `dao.stream.ws.md` (the WebSocket
     5	specification). Where this plan and the contract disagree, the contract
     6	wins. This document is transient: it is consumed as the phases complete,
     7	while the contract remains. Revised against the `gpt-5.6-sol` plan review
     8	of 2026-08-30 (`collab/review-dao-stream-plan.gpt-5.6-sol.stdout.log`).
     9	
    10	## Strategy
    11	
    12	Two deliberate narrowings:
    13	
    14	1. **A `dao.stream` namespace, not an in-place rewrite.** The legacy
    15	   `dao.stream` has 52 `open!` call sites and every subsystem as a
    16	   consumer; rewriting it in place is an atomic big bang. The new
    17	   namespace is `dao.stream` — one new file,
    18	   `src/cljc/dao/stream.cljc` — building the contract greenfield while
    19	   legacy keeps the system running.
    20	   Consumers migrate one at a time, later, under their own plans. The
    21	   legacy implementation and its tests are evidence about behavior, not
    22	   constraints: no compatibility facade, alias, or dual protocol.
    23	2. **The WebSocket slice, end to end, and nothing else.** One vertical
    24	   slice that forces the contract's riskiest promises early: the
    25	   descriptor round trip across a real serialization boundary, Model A
    26	   serving, writer-only surfaces, deposit admission, and observable
    27	   teardown. Other transports (file, UDP, log, relation, RPC) wait until
    28	   this slice stands.
    29	
    30	The slice still requires a minimal reader-surface core — the served stream
    31	and the deposit destination are both ring buffers — so the ring buffer is
    32	in scope, but only what the contract requires of it, nothing the legacy
    33	one happens to have.
    34	
    35	## Settled before Phase 1
    36	
    37	Three questions a phase would otherwise be forced to invent under delivery
    38	pressure. Each is decided here so no implementer decides it alone.
    39	
    40	**`attach!` is synchronous and never waits.** The Result Convention makes
    41	every operation return an outcome map; the contract's IO Model generalizes
    42	it — no operation waits. No transport in this slice returns a promise, a
    43	future, or a host-native async value from any of the seven operations, on
    44	any host. A ws `attach!` answers what is locally decidable (`ok`,
    45	`invalid-descriptor`) and how the connection resolved arrives as deposited
    46	lifecycle data and through the handle's own `append!` outcomes; see
    47	`dao.stream.ws.md`'s Operations section, corrected to match. A promise
    48	leaking out of a v2 operation is a defect, not a host accommodation.
    49	
    50	The rule covers `create!` equally, though this slice never exercises it:
    51	the ring buffer is in-memory and creates synchronously on every host, and
    52	the ws transport has no `create!` at all — it owns no stream, and starting
    53	an endpoint is a host composition step. A later transport whose creation
    54	needs host resources it cannot acquire synchronously (binding a port,
    55	opening a handle through an async-only API) answers the same way `attach!`
    56	does: the logical stream's identity exists at once, the handle reports
    57	acquisition state through its own operations, and the result of the
    58	acquisition arrives as data. Nothing waits.
    59	
    60	**Deposit admission is declared, never interrogated.** The contract's
    61	public surface has no way to ask a handle its retention policy, and must
    62	not gain one — a predicate answer about a live handle is exactly what
    63	`closed?` was retired for. So the boundary constructor takes the deposit
    64	destination *and its declared retention nature as data*, derived from the
    65	same creation specification that produced the handle, and refuses a
    66	reject-mode declaration at assembly. This is configuration provenance, not
    67	runtime introspection. Runtime behavior is unchanged: every non-`ok`
    68	deposit result tears the connection down.
    69	
    70	**Logical-stream identity is minted in Phase 2.** `cursor-mismatch` in
    71	Phase 2 and stable descriptor identity in Phase 3 are the same identity.
    72	It is allocated when a logical stream is created, and both cursors and
    73	descriptors denote it. Their *representations* need not match and need not
    74	be equally serializable — cursor serialization across hosts stays TBD per
    75	the contract — but they must refer to the same thing.
    76	
    77	## Phase 1 — Contract core (`src/cljc/dao/stream.cljc`)
    78	
    79	The protocol and data surface, with no transport. The seven operations are
    80	two kinds, and the split is the point:
    81	
    82	- **Five handle operations**, as protocols a handle implements per its
    83	  declared surface: `descriptor` (universal identity), `cursor` and `next`
    84	  (reader), `append!` (writer), `close!` (closable).
    85	- **Two transport entry functions**, `create!` and `attach!`, which take no
    86	  handle because none exists yet. These are per-transport functions.
    87	  Dynamic selection is a host-owned map from `:dao.stream/type` to a
    88	  `{create attach}` pair — ordinary data, ordinary lookup, performed by the
    89	  host. DaoStream ships no dispatch machinery: no multimethod, no registry,
    90	  no load-time side effect. Host dispatch is documentation plus an example
    91	  map in tests.
    92	
    93	Putting `create!` or `attach!` on a protocol is how the retired registry
    94	grows back; the file must not do it.
    95	
    96	Also in Phase 1:
    97	
    98	- The result convention: every operation returns an outcome map keyed by
    99	  `:dao.stream/outcome`, open maps, all qualified keywords under
   100	  `:dao.stream/…`, per-operation closed outcome sets exactly as the
   101	  contract's tables state (including the reserved `:dao.stream/denied` and
   102	  `:dao.stream/invalid-value`).
   103	- Generic envelope validation: `:dao.stream/type` present and qualified;
   104	  everything else transport-owned.
   105	- `descriptor` on every handle regardless of surface — identity is
   106	  universal and outlives an attachment, so it must not be bundled into the
   107	  reader or writer protocol.
   108	
   109	Deliverable: a contract-conformance test suite, **surface-aware by
   110	construction**. The harness takes a transport's factories plus its declared
   111	surface set and runs only the applicable blocks: shared laws (result
   112	shapes, envelope validation, descriptor identity, close scope) always;
   113	reader laws (cursor provenance — mint / successor / gap-recovery only —
   114	non-destructive multi-reader reads, anchors) only for handles declaring a
   115	reader surface; writer laws only for writers. A reader suite cannot run
   116	against a WebSocket handle, and a harness that assumes it can will be
   117	discovered in Phase 4 rather than here.
   118	
   119	The suite is *authored* in Phase 1 and first *demonstrated* in Phase 2.
   120	Phase 1 has no transport, so it proves nothing on its own; that is expected
   121	and is not a reason to defer writing it.
   122	
   123	## Phase 2 — Ring buffer reference (`src/cljc/dao/stream/ringbuffer.cljc`)
   124	
   125	The reference reader+writer+closable transport, scoped to the contract:
   126	
   127	- Logical-stream identity minted at `create!`, per *Settled before Phase 1*.
   128	- Evict-oldest retention with declared capacity; `append!` never returns
   129	  `full`; monotonic retention; `gap` with earliest-retained-else-tail
   130	  recovery cursor.
   131	- Cursor anchors `:dao.stream/oldest` and `:dao.stream/newest`;
   132	  `invalid-anchor`; cursors carry the logical-stream identity
   133	  (`cursor-mismatch` / `invalid-cursor` detectable); valid across handles
   134	  of the same logical stream.
   135	- One coherent state per operation (single atom; one deref per `next`),
   136	  satisfying the Concurrency section.
   137	
   138	Explicitly absent: waiters, drain, take, seq views, `closed?`, and **live
   139	resize** — resize is compatible with the contract but proves nothing the
   140	slice needs, and fixed declared capacity is sufficient for deposit
   141	admission. It follows the slice.
   142	
   143	The Phase 1 conformance suite runs here for the first time, with the full
   144	surface set. Ring-buffer-specific tests cover retention, anchors, and
   145	eviction reporting.
   146	
   147	## Phase 3 — Descriptor round trip
   148	
   149	The portable identity story, still in-memory, and constructible without
   150	WebSockets: the contract explicitly permits a host composition to keep a
   151	directory, which is what makes a ring buffer's descriptor resolvable on its
   152	own host.
   153	
   154	- `descriptor` on a ring buffer handle yields a plain-data envelope that
   155	  survives the transit codec structurally unchanged.
   156	- A **host-kept local directory** — a map from stream identity to live
   157	  handle, owned by the test composition, not by DaoStream and not by any
   158	  transport. It is deliberately test-only: the ws serving side in Phase 4
   159	  resolves `:ws/path` against its own served-stream table and does not
   160	  reuse this. Say so in the code, so nobody later mistakes it for
   161	  infrastructure.
   162	- Round-trip test: descriptor → encode → decode → `attach!` against that
   163	  composition → handle on the same logical stream; a kept cursor resumes
   164	  through the new handle. This is where the kept-cursor promise is proven,
   165	  on a transport that has a reader surface.
   166	- `not-found` for descriptors nothing backs.
   167	
   168	**Decision gate — descriptor key set.** The contract leaves the descriptor
   169	envelope TBD and this phase produces the evidence for settling it. Phase 4
   170	must not start against provisional keys: review the round trip's key set,
   171	settle it in `dao.stream.md`, and settle `:ws/…` in `dao.stream.ws.md`
   172	against it. Provisional keys carried into a wire format under delivery
   173	pressure become permanent by accident.
   174	
   175	## Phase 4 — WebSocket transport, forwarding, and serving composition
   176	
   177	Per `dao.stream.ws.md`, both ends. Three deliverables in three layers —
   178	transport, infrastructure, composition — deliberately separate:
   179	
   180	### 4a — The transport (`src/cljc/dao/stream/ws.cljc`)
   181	
   182	- **Client side**: `attach!` connects and returns without waiting (`ok` or
   183	  `invalid-descriptor` only — see *Settled before Phase 1*); the handle is
   184	  writer+closable only; `append!` with transient `full` (including while
   185	  the connection is still establishing), `invalid-value` for unencodable
   186	  values, `closed` once down; `close!` means disconnect, reattachable;
   187	  connection loss is the same detachment, uninvited.
   188	- **Boundary adapter**: deposits every inbound event — payload and
   189	  lifecycle, including how the connection resolved, judging nothing — into the
   190	  host-composed deposit destination, enforcing admission at assembly
   191	  against the declaration passed alongside the destination, and tearing the
   192	  connection down on any non-`ok` deposit result.
   193	- No callbacks in the public surface; the legacy `on-open!`/`on-message!`/
   194	  `on-close!` shape does not reappear.
   195	- **Dependency check**: `v2/ws.cljc` requires only `dao.stream` — never
   196	  the ringbuffer namespace. The ws transport depends on the protocols; the
   197	  ring buffer is the slice's composition partner (deposit destination,
   198	  served stream), wired by the host composition, not by the transport. A
   199	  transport requiring another transport is the legacy registry defect
   200	  reborn.
   201	
   202	### 4b — Forwarding (`src/cljc/dao/stream/forward.cljc`)
   203	
   204	Serving is what makes a stream remotely attachable, and nothing in the
   205	transport transfers a served stream's values to a connected socket. Under
   206	the contract that transfer is an interpreter: it reads one stream through
   207	its own cursor and appends to another. Streams notify no one, so nothing
   208	drives it — it polls, and when `next` answers `blocked` it schedules itself
   209	and asks again.
   210	
   211	That loop is not WebSocket-specific. It reads *any* reader handle and
   212	appends to *any* writer handle: ring buffer to socket, ring buffer to ring
   213	buffer, the code is identical. Its transport-agnosticism is the proof that
   214	it belongs neither inside a transport nor inside one composition. It is a
   215	third layer between them.
   216	
   217	`forward!` owns the three disciplines that are the same everywhere and are
   218	the easiest things in this slice to get wrong:
   219	
   220	- `full` **does not advance the cursor** — nothing was appended, and
   221	  treating the value as forwarded drops it silently;
   222	- `blocked` yields to the scheduler rather than spinning — on a cooperative
   223	  event loop a spin starves the runtime, so the send buffer never drains
   224	  and `full` never clears;
   225	- `end` closes the destination.
   226	
   227	It owns nothing else. The rest are arguments, because each has different
   228	right answers in different compositions:
   229	
   230	- **the scheduler** — "run this again later": `setTimeout` on Node, a
   231	  sleeping thread on the JVM. Cadence is the concern of the runtime driving
   232	  the interpreter, never of the stream (contract, *The readiness
   233	  extension*);
   234	- **the `gap` policy** — resume at the recovery cursor, or tear down. A
   235	  serving pump may skip evicted values; an interpreter that dropped
   236	  submitted work must not. Same outcome, opposite correct responses;
   237	- **the batch budget** — how many values to drain before yielding, which
   238	  exists only because a cooperative event loop needs yielding;
   239	- **starting it.** It never self-installs. The host wires it and starts it
   240	  explicitly, or implicit control flow is back.
   241	
   242	Testable with two ring buffers and no socket, so it may be written any time
   243	after Phase 2; only its *use* is Phase 4.
   244	
   245	### 4c — The serving composition (not in `v2/ws.cljc`, not in `forward.cljc`)
   246	
   247	What is left once the loop is infrastructure is the wiring and policy only
   248	the host has. This is a named deliverable, not test wiring, and it must
   249	define:
   250	
   251	- the serving lifecycle: what starts and stops an endpoint, and who owns it;
   252	- the `:ws/path` → served-stream resolution table, host-owned data;
   253	- the handshake that produces an authoritative `not-found` — the endpoint
   254	  responding and disclaiming — as distinct from a connection that never
   255	  completed;
   256	- how a connection is associated with the served stream it joined, and how
   257	  that identity reaches deposited events;
   258	- one `forward!` per connection, from the served stream to that
   259	  connection's ws handle, supplied with the scheduler, cadence, batch
   260	  budget, and `gap` policy this composition chooses;
   261	- whether inbound payload events are interpreted into appends on the served
   262	  stream, and if so by which interpreter — the boundary itself judges
   263	  nothing;
   264	- close behavior: what a server-side owner closing the logical stream does
   265	  to connected attachments.
   266	
   267	## Phase 5 — The slice, end to end
   268	
   269	Two processes, not two compositions in one. A same-process socket test is
   270	worth writing earlier for diagnostics, but it cannot show that a descriptor
   271	is self-contained or that no accidental shared state carries identity
   272	across — and those are central contract promises.
   273	
   274	Process A creates a ring buffer, serves it over ws, appends. Process B
   275	receives the descriptor as data, attaches, and appends outbound; B's host
   276	boundary deposits inbound traffic into B's medium; an interpreter in B
   277	reads that medium with cursors. State the bootstrap channel explicitly:
   278	how B obtains A's descriptor is part of the test, not an assumption.
   279	
   280	Kill the connection, then verify three separable facts:
   281	
   282	1. Detachment is observable on both sides — B's handle answers `closed`,
   283	   A's boundary deposits the departure.
   284	2. The served stream survives on A, and `attach!` with the same descriptor
   285	   rejoins the same logical stream.
   286	3. B's cursor **on B's deposit medium** still resumes — that medium
   287	   outlives the socket. The cursor is not handed to `attach!` and not used
   288	   through the ws handle, which has no reader surface. WebSocket-level
   289	   resumption — what history a rejoining client receives — is deferred in
   290	   `dao.stream.ws.md` and is not tested here.
   291	
   292	## Host matrix
   293	
   294	- **Phases 1–3** are pure `.cljc` with no transport: they must compile and
   295	  pass on clj, cljs (Node), and cljd. Nothing in them is host-specific, and
   296	  a parity break here is cheapest to find here.
   297	- **Phase 4–5** target clj and cljs (Node) — Node server plus Node client is
   298	  the slice. Browser-client behavior is not validated by a Node target and
   299	  is out of the slice. cljd ws follows once the slice is proven; beware the
   300	  `#?(:cljd nil :clj ...)` reader-conditional trap.
   301	- **`forward.cljc` is host-agnostic** — its only host dependency is the
   302	  scheduler handed to it — so it compiles and its ring-buffer-to-ring-buffer
   303	  tests pass on all three hosts, including where the ws transport does not
   304	  yet exist.
   305	- Per host, name explicitly: how transient send-buffer `full` is detected,
   306	  how a send racing a concurrent close is handled, and that the codec
   307	  produces structurally identical values across all three.
   308	
   309	## Boundary of this plan
   310	
   311	Not in this plan, by design:
   312	
   313	- Migration of any consumer (dao.runtime, yin.vm, dao.jing, dao.space)
   314	  — each gets its own plan against the finished slice.
   315	- Other transports (file, UDP, log, relation, apply/RPC layers).
   316	- The waiter/readiness extension, Shibi, ws resumption protocol, cursor
   317	  serialization across hosts, live ring buffer resize — deferred in the
   318	  contract, deferred here.
   319	
   320	## End condition — v2 is not a permanent fork
   321	
   322	The v2 namespace exists to protect the running system during migration,
   323	not to live forever. The slice is complete when Phases 1–5 pass on clj and
   324	cljs; it is explicitly **incomplete on cljd** until the cljd ws transport
   325	lands, and the end condition is not met before then.
   326	
   327	When the slice is complete and the last consumer has migrated (under its
   328	own plan), legacy `dao.stream` is deleted and a single decision is taken
   329	explicitly: `dao.stream` is renamed to `dao.stream`, or keeps its name
   330	permanently. An undecided coexistence of both namespaces is a defect of the
   331	migration, not a steady state.

